MASTER PROMPT — AI-ASSISTED PROPOSAL REPORT ADAPTATION ENGINE

PROJECT GOAL

Build an AI-assisted application that automates the existing manual Proposal/report development workflow.

This is NOT a generic PDF-to-RDLC converter.

The real workflow is:

Existing Proposal Product
→ find similar benchmark product
→ copy benchmark
→ rename ProductCode/PlanCode
→ modify ProposalBuilder / ProposalElement / RDLC
→ handle ProductEngine-dependent values with controlled mock data when ProductEngine is unavailable
→ run report with representative data
→ validate/test
→ preview PDF
→ compare against target/reference
→ approve
→ insert generated files into the real project

The application should make this workflow faster, safer, repeatable, testable, and traceable.


==================================================
1. CORE ARCHITECTURAL PRINCIPLE
==================================================

AI decides WHAT should change.

Deterministic code decides HOW files are changed.

AI must NOT directly freestyle-edit the real project.

The controlled pipeline is:

ANALYZE
→ DECIDE
→ GENERATE IN STAGING
→ TEST
→ PREVIEW
→ VALIDATE
→ USER APPROVAL
→ CLI DRY RUN
→ CLI INSERT
→ REAL PROJECT

The real project must remain untouched until explicit user approval and CLI insertion.


==================================================
2. EXISTING PROJECT IS THE SOURCE OF TRUTH
==================================================

The existing Proposal project is the strongest source of truth.

Do NOT redesign the existing Proposal architecture unnecessarily.

The generated result should look like a developer took an existing benchmark product, copied it, renamed it, and adapted it — not like AI invented a completely new architecture.

Inspect:

- ProposalBuilder.vb
- ProposalElement.vb
- ProposalLayout.rdlc
- related files
- datasets
- parameters
- report expressions
- naming conventions
- ProductEngine dependencies
- current mock/comment strategy
- project references
- project file structure
- existing report execution patterns
- existing test/debugging patterns


==================================================
3. TWO PDF INPUTS
==================================================

The application accepts TWO PDF files.

PDF A — NEW PRODUCT TEMPLATE

This is the target report/template for the new product.

It represents the desired visual and structural output.

Analyze:

- pages
- sections
- tables
- labels
- dynamic fields
- status fields
- layout
- formatting
- images
- spacing
- structure
- repeated elements
- page-level differences


PDF B — EXISTING SYSTEM GENERATED PDF

This is a PDF generated by the current existing system.

It should be treated as:

1. Golden/reference output
2. Visual reference
3. Source of realistic representative data
4. Source for candidate dummy/test data

Example extracted data:

{
  "Customer": "Budi",
  "PolicyNumber": "POL001",
  "Premium": 750000,
  "Status": "ACTIVE",
  "BenefitAmount": 10000000
}

Every extracted value should have traceability to:

- PDF file
- page
- region/bounding box where possible
- detected label/context
- confidence

If semantics are uncertain:

- mark the field uncertain
- provide confidence
- do not silently guess
- allow manual confirmation

Do NOT assume every PDF value can be perfectly reconstructed.


==================================================
4. WHY PDF B IS IMPORTANT
==================================================

PDF B is not merely a visual comparison file.

It can provide representative data for executing the generated report.

The purpose is:

Existing System PDF
→ Extract representative values
→ Build deterministic dummy/reference fixture
→ Execute generated ProposalBuilder
→ Build dataset
→ Execute generated RDLC
→ Render generated PDF
→ Compare output

This allows report development even when ProductEngine is not yet complete.

ProductEngine not being finished must NOT become a blocker for report development.


